When this temperature value is reached, the change in chemical free energy provides sufficient thermodynamic driving force to overcome martensite nucleation barriers. [ 18 ]  Above Ms, the transformation can occur if it is assisted by mechanical energy (stress or strain), which provides an additional driving force to reach the critical activation energy for transformation. This is known as stress-assisted or strain-induced martensitic transformation. For mechanically assisted transformation to occur, the material must be below the Md temperature. Angel  et al. [ 19 ]  proposed a Md30 temperature, defined as the temperature at which 50 pct  α ′-martensite is produced after 30 pct of true deformation under tension. Subsequent studies [ 20 ]  included additional elements and austenitic grain size (GS) in Angel´s correlation, resulting in the formula presented in Eq. [ 2 ]. 2 Md3 0 = 551 - 462 pctC + pctN - 9 . 2pctSi - 8 . 1pctMn - 13 . 7pctCr - 29 pctNi + pctCu - 18 . 5pctMo - 68 pctNb - 1.42 ASTM GS - 8 \documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
