Martensite content in the samples subjected to tensile test was analyzed using the ferritometric method. A FMP30e Fischer equipment was employed to take measurements; readings were acquired starting 3 mm away from the fracture zone recording the average of 10 data points. Measurements were then taken at various distances from the fracture surface (5, 10, 15, and 20 mm). The ferritometric method is a non-destructive subsurface technique ideally suited for mapping the  α ′-martensite phase gradient that varies as a function of distance from the fracture surface. Since the equipment provides a measurement of the percentage of δ-ferrite and not  α ′-martensite, a conversion factor from  δ -ferrite to  α ′-martensite, as determined by Talonen  et al. [ 28 ]  and which suitability for quantitative magnetic phase analysis was supported by Beese, [ 29 ]  was utilized (see Eq. [ 3 ]): 3 α ′ - martensite wt pct = 1.7 × F \documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
