Figure  4  shows the martensite content as a function of the distance from the fracture surface for the AISI 304L tensile specimens. Martensite content decreases as the distance from the fracture surface and necking zone increases, evidencing the deformation-induced  α ′-martensite formation mechanism. The samples tested at 10 °C exhibit the biggest amount of martensite, particularly in the necking region. The martensite content for the samples tested at 10 °C and a strain rate of 8.3 × 10 −4  s −1  reaches values beyond the limit of reliability for Talonen’s formula. Therefore, they are shown in the graph as a reference to complete the sample set. For a given temperature, the martensite content increases as the strain rate decreases. Likewise, for a given speed, the amount of martensite increases as the temperature decreases. [ 18 , 21 , 37 ]  The obtained curves for the whole set of samples were fit to a potential curve with a negative exponent (Eq. [ 4 ]). The exponent  n  and the coefficient  A  are empirical constants that depend on the material and testing conditions. The equation allows for a precise estimation of the amount of  α ′-martensite formed at any distance from the fracture zone ( d ), with a very good precision ( R 2  ≥ 0.9). 4 α ′ = A d n \documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
